```typescript
function readPlace(value: unknown): string {
  if (typeof value !== "object" || value === null) return "No place";
  if (!("place" in value)) return "No place";
  if (typeof value.place !== "string") return "No place";
  return value.place;
}
console.log(readPlace({ place: "Hill" }));
console.log(readPlace({ other: "Hill" }));
```
This prints Hill, then No place. readPlace takes an unchecked value. typeof checks its runtime kind; !== means not equal; || means either failure is enough. null needs its own check because typeof null is "object". "place" in value checks the name. ! negates that result. Each early return stops unsafe later reads; the last return is reached only for a string.

